# OpenGrid places — the value space of dct:spatial on a record.
#
# Small on purpose. This is not a gazetteer and must never grow into one: it
# holds the places the Hub's own records actually name, so that "is there a
# reference network for here" is a question with a stable key rather than a
# string match on a label. Everything else about a place — its borders, its
# population, its alternative names — belongs to the authority each concept
# points at, and this scheme's job is to point rather than to restate.
#
# ## The authority is Wikidata, and ISO is carried only where it is true
#
# Every concept carries skos:exactMatch to a Wikidata entity. That is the
# external identifier, and it is what makes these concepts joinable to anything
# outside this repository.
#
# skos:notation carries an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code **only where the place is
# that country**. This matters more than it sounds. The obvious thing to do
# with the Great Britain reference model is to file it under ISO "GB", and ISO
# "GB" is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland — which
# includes the one thing that model explicitly excludes. A picker offering
# "United Kingdom" and returning a network with no Northern Ireland in it is
# an over-claim of exactly the kind this branch exists not to make, and it is
# invisible to a reader who does not already know the model's exclusions.
#
# So Great Britain carries no ISO notation. It is the island, Wikidata says so,
# and the scheme declines to equate it with the state.
#
# ## Adding a place
#
# A concept here is a claim that the Hub has something to say about somewhere.
# Add one when a record names it, not in anticipation: a scheme listing every
# country the Hub *could* cover reads as a survey of what exists, which is a far
# stronger claim than this catalog is entitled to make. What the Hub has not
# built is said in the interface, in those words, and is not encoded here as an
# absence.
#
# IRIs follow docs/conventions.md: scheme
# https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place, concept tokens lowerCamelCase.

@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix pl:   <https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place/> .
@prefix wd:   <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/> .

<https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place>
  a skos:ConceptScheme ;
  dct:title "OpenGrid Places"@en ;
  dct:description """Places the Hub's records name, used as the value space of dct:spatial. Each concept is identified by a Wikidata entity, which is the authority; skos:notation carries an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code only where the concept is that country, so that a sub-national or cross-border area is never filed under a state it is not. The scheme holds what the catalog describes and nothing in anticipation of it."""@en ;
  dct:issued "2026-09-13"^^xsd:date ;
  owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ;
  skos:hasTopConcept pl:greatBritain, pl:germany .

pl:greatBritain
  a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place> ;
  skos:topConceptOf <https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place> ;
  skos:prefLabel "Great Britain"@en ;
  skos:altLabel "GB mainland"@en ;
  skos:exactMatch wd:Q23666 ;
  skos:definition "The island comprising England, Scotland and Wales, together with the transmission network synchronised across it."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote """Deliberately not ISO 3166-1 "GB", which is the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. Northern Ireland is a separate synchronous area operating with Ireland, and the reference network filed here excludes it. Filing this under the state's code would tell a reader they were getting a UK network and hand them one with a nation missing."""@en .

pl:germany
  a skos:Concept ;
  skos:inScheme <https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place> ;
  skos:topConceptOf <https://schema.opengrid.org/concept/place> ;
  skos:prefLabel "Germany"@en ;
  skos:notation "DE" ;
  skos:exactMatch wd:Q183 ;
  skos:definition "The Federal Republic of Germany, whose transmission network is operated by four TSOs within one synchronous area."@en ;
  skos:scopeNote """ISO 3166-1 "DE" is exact here: the network filed for this place is the country's, with no sub-national exclusion. The interconnectors to its nine neighbours are outside it, which is a statement about the model and not about the place."""@en .
